Performance Comparisons Between the DSP 100 and Other Amateur HF Radios}}} {\cs16\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} {\f5 Performance Comparisons Between the DSP 100 and Other Amateur HF Radios}}}{\fs44 
Performance Comparisons Between the DSP 100 and Other Amateur HF Radios.
\par }\pard \qc {\fs44 
\par }\pard {\f5 \tab The DSP 100 is not directly comparable to
 any transceiver on the market. The major areas of performance differences are as follows. Noise performance of the DSP 100 is better due to the lack of narrow band filters ahead of the digital signal processor. In general the DSP 100 with its fairly high
 second and third order intercept points can handle QRM better. The DSP 100 can suffer when subjected to very large AM signals and the mixers become saturated. This typical only occurs in the AM broadcast bands in close proximity to high powered stations.
 T
he selectivity of the DSP 100 is derived from its 16 bit DSP software which produces filters of the performance shown under filters in the RADWARE software help files of versions 1.17 and later. The 64 times oversampling of the A to D converter  produces 
a filter ahead of the DSP that is typically flat at 7.5 KHz bandwidth and 80 dB down at 8.6 KHz bandwidth. The sensitivity of the DSP 100 is 1 }{\f5 {\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 109 \\f "Symbol" \\s 10}{\fldrslt\f1\fs20}}}{\f5 
Volt for 10 dB signal to noise. This sensitivity is not as good as most receivers a
vailable today. However, the DSP 100 was designed purposely with this sensitivity as it is more than adequate when used with a good fixed station antenna. An increase in the sensitivity would reduce the dynamic range. 
\par 
\par   
\par }\pard \qc {\fs44 \page }{\cs16\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super #} {\f5 Advantages_and_Disadvantages_of_the_DSP_100_to_Conventional_Transceivers}}} {\cs16\super ${\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super $} {\f5 
Advantages and Disadvantages of the DSP 100 to Conventional Transceivers}}} {\cs16\super K{\footnote \pard\plain \s15 \f4\fs20 {\cs16\super K} {\f5 Advantages and Disadvantages of the DSP 100 to Conventional Transceivers}}}{\fs44 
Advantages and Disadvantages of the DSP 100 to Conventional Transceivers
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f5\fs18 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}{\f5\fs18 \tab The assumptions made here are that the standard HF radio of 1995 is a superhetrodyne with three IF\rquote 
s, one around 45 MHz, one around 5 MHz and a final IF of 455 KHz. The superhetrodyne  became the standard architecture for radio receivers in the 1940\rquote s.  
At this time AM was the main voice communication mode with CW carrying the majority of message communications. The difficulties of  obtaining sufficient selectivity without using a superhetrodyne system were enormous. For CW, and with the
 advent of SSB, direct conversion (homodyne) has been used but has remained in the background as a low cost, low performance system. The problems with Direct conversion have been as follows:
\par }\pard \ri-1440 {\f5\fs18 1    Difficulty of reliably producing wide band (300- 3000 Hz) phaseshift networks.
\par 2    Microphonics due to the very large audio gain used.
\par 3    The complexity of implementing systems such as the Weaver method in hardware.
\par 4    Difficulties of building high performance audio filters.
\par 5    The subtractive second order products and envelope detection in the mixers cause overload of the system when very large AM or SSB signals are present.
\par 
\par Problems 1, through 4  become much easier when implemented by software in a DSP system. In fact problem 4 can be solved with DSP filters that far exceed analog filtering at any stage. Problem 5 remains a hardware limitation. The DSP 100 uses integrated ci
rcuit mixers with high second order intercept points. The use of these devices considerably increases the dynamic range of the receiver over that of other direct conversion systems.  
\par \tab The 
superhetrodyne receiver has its own set of problems. Obviously the introduction of other sensitive frequencies within the system raise the possibility of the reception of unwanted signals. These,  of cause are what the specifications of IF and image
 rejection are concerned with.
\par \tab High first IF receivers also require very low phase noise oscillators.  As an example consider a receiver operating on 7 MHz. In the case of 
a direct conversion receiver an oscillator of 7 MHz would be used. For a superhetrodyne receiver with a 45 MHz first IF the local oscillator would be at 52 MHz which is about 7.5 times the direct conversion case. Phase noise increases as the square of the
 frequency increase. Therefore if the same circuit were used in both cases the superhetrodyne phase noise would be 55 times or  17 dB worse.
\par \tab A superhetrodyne receiver with narrow filters ahead of any noise blanking or  suppression techniques has a serious 
disadvantage. When a narrow, high amplitude pulse is applied to a narrow band filter, the pulse is converted into a damped sine wave centered on the filter frequency. The processes of removing this noise has become very complex even with the best digital 
processing. The more narrow band filters used within the system the closer the noise looks to a wanted signal. 
\par \tab Pass band tuning is not specifically a problem that arises from the use of a superhetrodyne receiver. By off setting two of the IFs in a multi I
F system it is possible to produce a variable bandwidth system. The Figure shows two IF filters of the same bandwidth. If both filters are aligned the bandwidth shown in green results. Assuming that both filters have a 3 to 60 dB shape factor of 1 to 3 th
e resulting shape factor would be close 1 to 1.5 for the same  3 to 60 dB points. If the result of sliding one filter away from the other is looked at (the overlap section of both filters shown in blue to the side) the shape factor  is now very poor.  Whi
ls
t this is undoubtedly a cheap way of achieve some sort of variable selectivity the results are poor when compared to selecting a more optimum filter. In a crude sense the more IFs in a receiver design the lower the performance. To insert additional IFs to
 obtain poor quality selectivity is not ideal. 
\par 
\par }
