\par 
\par }\pard {\f5 \tab 
There are no narrow band filters ahead if the analog to digital converter in the DSP 100. The DSP software can therefore distinguish between signals and pulse type noise very easily. First a very large short pulse is simply a one or two sample overload t
o the A to D converter, and a significant suppression of this noise is easy to implement.  
Secondly the complete bandwidth of the A to D converter can be processed at the same time as the required narrow band signal, allowing more options to the removal of the noise.
\par \tab The  audio phase shifts are mathematically implemented with Hilbert Transforms in the DSP software. This is simpler and is 100 percent repeatable, which certainly is not the case with RC phase shift networks.
\par \tab The DSP filters that provide the bulk of the selectivity use only about 160 bytes of memory. This allows large numbers of fi
lters to be saved and an ideal filter selected for a given situation.    Other than the noise issues this is probably the most important performance advantage from moving DSP closer to the front end of the receiver. In an analog receiver, optimum performa
nce would be obtained by having an ideal bandwidth and shape factor filter, for each mode and bandwidth used. This quickly gets very expensive but is exactly what happens with DSP receiver at a very minimal cost.
