Computer technology is improving at an incredible rate. It would be very easy to build a single card equivalent of the DSP 100. However for this receiver / exciter, the expensive part of the system is still the analog sections, mixers, bandpass filters a
nd synthesizer (partly digital). If the DSP circuits were built onto the same card as the analog sections, when a new DSP processor comes along
, the analog section would need to be scraped.  With the two card system, when a faster DSP card is available, the performance of the whole system can be improved at a minimum cost. The Current DSP cards used in the DSP 100 system are sold as Sound Blaste
r Compatible }{\f5\fs18 {\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 226 \\f "Symbol" \\s 9}{\fldrslt\f1\fs18}}}{\f5\fs18 
 sound cards.  They are not, however, Sound Blaster cards. The basic Sound Blaster set up uses a SoundPort Stereo Codec which is hooked up to the computer bus.  The stereo codec is basically a dual analog to digita
l converter (A to D) and dual digital to analog converter (D to A) with parallel interfaces.  To use this system the computer reads the A to Ds, then the system processor, 386, 486, pentium etc., operates on this data and writes to the D to As. The system
 processor is not a very good Digital Signal Processor, and even if it were, having it perform the computations necessary to build a high performance radio receiver would grind the fastest system to a halt. Enter the cards used by the DSP 100 system. Thes
e 
cards have an applications specific integrated circuit (ASIC) which is placed between the system bus and the codec. Attached to the ASIC is also a Digital Signal Processor with 16K by 24 bits of memory. Under computer control the ASIC can switch to Sound 
Blaster compatibility, Load the DSP memory, give the Codec to the DSP, or read the DSP memory. To build a radio from this system all that is required is to load the DSP memory with the program for a radio, and give control to the DSP.   There is also hand
shaking between the system and DSP processor to keep everything in control. The above is only a basic outline of how the system works. It is beyond the intention of this section to cover this precisely. 
\par \tab 
An ISA bus card has been used for a number of reasons. There are many controls that need to be on the system bus to enable high speed control for example frequency. When systems are mounted outside of the computer cable lengths and placements can often c
reate problems with noise. With the card inside the com
puter these are all under control, and with good screening the noise and interference from the computer can be eliminated. There are many special applications for these cards where they would work in conjunction with singe board ISA bus computers. 
\par \tab 
The DSP 100 could not be built with a high output power on the card inside the computer as the heat generated by the power amplifier would be excessive. An external power supply would also be required. As the power amplifier could not be built inside the
 computer it was decided to minimize the transmitter parts used on the card so that it could be purchased as a receiver without charges for unused transmitter parts. 
\par \tab There will undoubtedly be  analog sections other than the DSP 100 marketed by COMER that have other frequency ranges.   All systems will use the same basic software with variations taken into account by the configuration menu.
\par  \tab COMER has chosen to provide free software upgrades for the DSP 100. These free upgrades are for the general control and DSP
 software used by the system. It may be that some special applications software not related to consumer application could involve additional costs. It must also be understood that this software is copyright Comer Communications Inc. for use only for demon
stration or operation with Comer Communications Inc. products. The use of this software for applications not involving these products is strictly forbidden. Comer Communications does not guarantee that this software will continue to be free to future purc
